import sys
p = sys.argv[1]
lines = open(p, encoding='utf-8').read().split('\n')
idx = [i for i, l in enumerate(lines) if l.startswith('| 614 |')]
assert len(idx) == 1
lines.insert(idx[0] + 1, "| 615 | 2026-10-21 | **Modes: five lessons on Home, Pitch, Modes.** Her request (2026-10-21): make all the modes lessons, then check them together; taught as a lecture to her, since she says she herself is unsure of the mode concepts. The concept page now has: 1 \"The same white keys, a new home\" (`md-start`: C major's white keys, move the home to any white key, then tap the notes), 2 \"The seven modes\" (`sc-modes`, now the `MdNames` component: the seven homes on the white keys of C with names, mood, example, and a question: tap the home of a named mode; a wrong tap says so and counts the white keys from C), 3 \"The same home, a new colour\" (`md-colour`: C major above, the chosen mode on C below, the changed notes in coral, with a line saying which degrees were lowered or raised), 4 \"Find the parent major\" (`md-parent`: three steps on E Dorian, count back to the first note, then eight questions with four choices; a wrong choice says so and shows the count back), 5 \"Modes in any key\" (`md-keys`: choose mode and home note, the parent major and its key signature are worked out, staff on request, then play it yourself). No new metaphors; the facts are the MODES table (her material). **For her review (mine, not from her material):** the parent-major rule (the home note of the nth mode is the nth note of its parent major, so count back n-1 notes), the way each mode is compared with C major (which degrees are lowered or raised, derived from the parent scale's spelling), the ordinals and the Korean wording. The old `ScModes` component (with parent chips) is no longer used and stays until the chapter rebuild. Built by `patch625.py` (+ `md_block.jsx`) (`t628`). Tested all five lessons at 390 px in English and the Home page in Korean: no console errors. Local commit only, not pushed. |")
open(p, 'w', encoding='utf-8').write('\n'.join(lines)); print('ok')
